"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 7.8. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3), pT2a stage, positive surgical margins, and absence of seminal vesicle invasion, lymphovascular invasion, and lymph node metastasis. A key finding is the discrepancy between the biopsy grade (Gleason 4+3, ISUP grade group 2) and the prostatectomy specimen grade (ISUP grade group 3). The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding.\n\nCountervailing factors include the positive surgical margins and the higher pathological grade observed in the final specimen compared to the biopsy. Missing postoperative PSA measurements or follow-up data significantly widen the uncertainty regarding the trajectory of the postoperative PSA. Information regarding the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trend is crucial for refining this prognosis. The current heuristic sensitivity range for recurrence is between 43 and 71 months. The fixed estimated horizon is 56.82 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 42.61–71.02 months; its coverage has not been validated."
